        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/technical-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Man’s waistcoat with a round neckline, curving fronts and skirts reaching to the top of the thigh.  Each front has a pocket and rectangular pocket flap.  The fronts and back are made of pale pink silk satin.  The pocket flaps are lined with ivory silk twill, the pockets with linen.  The waistcoat is embroidered-to-shape with tamboured silk thread in white, yellow and shades of green, pink and terracotta, in a pattern of flowers, swags, tassels and narrow border on the pocket flaps, below the pockets, around the front neck, edges and hems.  There are 11 embroidered buttons on the right front.\n\nThe waistcoat was altered in the late 19th century, for fancy dress or a studio prop.  Grey cotton was inserted into the centre-back seam and linen tape ties sewn to the back.  The buttonholes may have originally been on a strip under the left front; 11 buttonholes were later cut through the embroidery.  The original lining appears to have been unpicked, padding added to the front and the lining resewn."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/b77bcc2b-107c-3253-bf5a-41ab5d6ce5af> .

